    "body": "Stacked on #39496.\r\nCloses #39434.\r\n\r\n### Problem\r\n\r\n`vim.ui.img` emits kitty graphics protocol termcodes directly to the TTY, so UIs that are not terminals (Neovide, goneovim, fvim, ...) cannot display images (#39434).\r\n\r\n### Solution\r\n\r\nAdd an experimental, opt-in \"ext_images\" UI capability with three events, copying the general structure of the kitty graphics protocol (transmit / place / delete) while staying provider-agnostic:\r\n\r\n- **img_data(id, data, opts):** transmit raw PNG bytes, once per image. Provides an `id` managed by neovim and the raw `data` representing the bytes of the image. `opts` is the extension point for future chunked/streamed transfer of large payloads, or other stuff.\r\n- **img_set(id, opts):** display/update an image by `id` without retransmitting its data. Either a direct placement `{row, col, width, height, zindex}` (0-based cells on the global layout), or `{virtual=true, width, height}` where the image is rendered at U+10EEEE placeholder cells carried by the normal grid, so editor and buffer relative placements scroll, clip, and stack like normal.\r\n- img_del(id): remove the image and free the data.\r\n\r\n`vim.ui.img` gains a second internal backend found in `_events.lua`, implementing the same interface as the kitty backend. When an attached UI activated `ext_images`, new images use UI events. Otherwise, we continue to use kitty. At the moment, I also have each image keeping the backend that created it, meaning that I'm keeping track of the backend per image.\r\n\r\nEvents are emitted from Lua via private API functions (`nvim__ui_img_data`/`nvim__ui_img_set`/`nvim__ui_img_del`). Oh, and `ext_images` implies `ext_linegrid`, since virtual placements are rendered from grid cells.\r\n\r\nI haven't added chunking transfer of large images, and we still aren't handling fractional pixels and stuff.\r\n\n",
